1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
This is Task 9, Selenium test design.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Selenium is a tool that controls a real web browser from code: it opens pages, types in fields, clicks buttons and reads what is on the screen, like a very fast, very careful user.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
We chose five critical user journeys.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Here are the five test functions: register, login with wrong and right password, search request and confirm, cancel and track, and a booked room being unavailable to another guest.

5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
WebDriver is the standard way the test code talks to the browser.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Here is the fixture that creates the browser for each test.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
It starts Chrome, headless by default, which means without a visible window, and that is how Jenkins runs it.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Setting HEADLESS to zero shows the window.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
It gets the driver program from Selenium itself, and it quits the browser after every test.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
If a test fails, it also saves a screenshot.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Test data must not make tests depend on each other.

12
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Every test registers a new guest with a random email built from a UUID, and uses dates far in the future that change for each run.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
So tests never clash, even against a deployment that already has old bookings.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
The password is a fixed value that only exists for tests.

15
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
A locator tells Selenium which element on the page to use.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
We use ids, such as full name, email and register button.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Ids are the most stable locator, because the developers put them in the page on purpose, and they do not change when the design changes.

18
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:02,000
The helper function register shows the pattern: find the element, type into it, click, then wait for the result.

19
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Web pages take time to respond, so a test must wait, but not blindly.

20
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
We never use fixed sleeps.

21
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
We use explicit waits: wait until this element is visible, or until this text appears, for at most ten seconds.

22
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
As soon as the condition is true, the test continues.

23
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
The fixture even sets the implicit wait to zero so that only our explicit waits apply.

24
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
This makes tests fast and stable.

25
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
An assertion is the check that decides pass or fail.

26
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Here is journey three.

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:53,000
After searching and booking, it asserts that the status is PENDING and that the dates on the page equal the dates I chose.

28
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:02,000
After clicking Confirm, it waits for CONFIRMED and asserts that the message says booking confirmed, and that the confirm button is gone.

29
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
If any assertion is false, the test fails and the pipeline stops.

30
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,000
When a browser test fails, text alone is hard to understand, so the fixture saves a screenshot and the page source.

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
This is the function.

32
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
These files are real ones from earlier deliberate failure experiments, with their dates.

33
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:30,000
In Jenkins they are archived with the build.

34
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
A passing run creates none.

35
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Now I run the browser tests with the documented command.

36
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Pytest starts the real application on a free port, drives headless Chrome through the five journeys, and prints one line per test.

37
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:58,000
I also ask for a JUnit XML report, the format Jenkins reads, and then print its summary: tests, failures and errors.

38
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
The next video part shows the same tests in a visible browser.

39
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
How do the three kinds of test differ?

40
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Unit tests check one function or class, in isolation, in milliseconds.

41
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Integration tests send requests to the Flask app without a browser and check routes together with the database.

42
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Selenium tests use a real browser through the whole stack, so they are slowest, but they prove that a real user can complete a journey.

43
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Here are the real counts of each kind in this project.


